# Server rendering and client hydration

An SSR application runs across two worlds: first on the server, where a `Request` becomes an HTML document with embedded `Flags`; then in the browser, where Foldkit attaches event listeners and stream mounts to the existing DOM without discarding the server-rendered markup.

Because the server entry and the client entry are the two halves of one handshake, this chapter keeps them side by side and tangles them into `src/entry.server.ts` and `src/entry.ts`.

## Rendering the document on the server

Foldkit's `@foldkit/vite-plugin` calls `src/entry.server.ts` in three situations: during `vite dev` for every incoming page or API request, during `vite build` to pre-render every route listed in `prerenderPaths` into static HTML, and inside `src/server/bunServer.ts` when the standalone Bun server renders a dynamic page.

`renderDocument` wraps the rendered application markup and asset tags in a full HTML shell with `lang="en"` and a dark slate `theme-color` meta tag.

`renderPage` runs an Effect program provided with `TownServiceLive`. If the request is an `OPTIONS` preflight, it returns a `204 No Content` response via `Server.Responded`. Otherwise, it checks `handleApiRequestEffect(request)` first so `/api/town/*` calls work seamlessly inside `vite dev` as well as in production. For page requests, it asks `TownService` to resolve the initial `TownSnapshot` from URL query parameters and the `borgo_solare_prefs` cookie, renders `{ Flags, init, view }` to string via `Server.renderToString`, and attaches `cache-control: private, no-store` whenever a cookie personalized the response.

=>

import { Effect, Option } from 'effect'
import { Server } from 'foldkit/experimental'

import { Flags, init, view } from './main'
import {
  TownService,
  TownServiceLive,
  handleApiRequestEffect,
} from './server/townService'

export const prerenderPaths = ['/']

export const renderDocument: Server.DocumentRenderer = (application, assets) =>
  Server.renderDocument(application, assets, {
    lang: 'en',
    head: '<meta name="theme-color" content="#0f172a">',
  })

const renderPageEffect = (request: Request) =>
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    if (request.method === 'OPTIONS') {
      return Server.Responded(
        new Response(null, {
          status: 204,
          headers: {
            allow: 'GET, HEAD, POST, OPTIONS',
          },
        }),
      )
    }

    const maybeApiResponse = yield* handleApiRequestEffect(request)
    if (Option.isSome(maybeApiResponse)) {
      return Server.Responded(maybeApiResponse.value)
    }

    const townService = yield* TownService
    const flags = yield* townService.resolveSnapshotForRequest(request)

    const renderedApplication = yield* Server.renderToString(
      { Flags, init, view },
      { flags },
    )

    return Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(request.headers.get('cookie')), {
      onNone: () => Server.Rendered(renderedApplication),
      onSome: () =>
        Server.Rendered(renderedApplication, {
          headers: {
            'cache-control': 'private, no-store',
            vary: 'cookie',
          },
        }),
    })
  })

export const renderPage = (request: Request): Promise<Server.EntryResult> =>
  Effect.runPromise(
    renderPageEffect(request).pipe(Effect.provide(TownServiceLive)),
  )

## Hydrating on the client

Once the HTML document reaches the browser, `src/entry.ts` constructs the Foldkit runtime application from `Model`, `Flags`, `init`, `update`, `view`, and `subscriptions`, targeting `#root`. Calling `Runtime.hydrate(application)` decodes the server-serialized `Flags`, reconciles the virtual DOM against `#root`, and activates `MountTownViewport` — bringing the Three.js promontory to life inside the already-rendered HUD frame.

=>

import { Runtime } from 'foldkit'

import { Flags, Model, init, subscriptions, update, view } from './main'
import './styles.css'

const application = Runtime.makeApplication({
  Model,
  Flags,
  init,
  update,
  view,
  subscriptions,
  container: document.getElementById('root'),
})

Runtime.hydrate(application)

